# DEC-730's `delete_with` said a test-only method's spec code tests nothing
# live, but the hunt found it on rows whose own grading says the method may
# be called (a helper Rails mixes into a view, a name config holds, a module
# listed at runtime): deleting those specs loses a test of live code. Only a
# `clear` row plans units.
dead lib Lowered#shine=test-only&~public_send
# What the method reaches was never weighed: `initialize` was exempt
# however much it ran, and the method's own body could call live code or
# read a live constant, so its spec was the only test of that code. Each
# body is weighed now, and one that reaches live code yields no unit.
dead lib Tally#total=test-only& Mention#rewrite=test-only& Mention#slug=test-only&
# Setup that runs before the examples was weighed only as `before`,
# `around`, `let!` and `subject!`: `before_all`, test-prof's `let_it_be`
# and discourse's `fab!` ran live code unweighed. Each is a hook now, as is
# any call named with a `!` that takes a block.
dead lib Booter#first=test-only& Booter#second=test-only& Booter#third=test-only&
# A call in a group's body that is no RSpec DSL — a helper's macro that
# defines a `before` (`with_registry`) — was weighed for a group unit, not
# for an example in that group. Each such statement of a group around the
# unit is weighed as a hook now.
dead lib Labeled#label=test-only&
# A unit is deleted by its lines, so an example sharing a line with
# another (`it {…}; it {…}`) took that one with it. A unit any other
# statement shares a line with is no unit.
dead lib Oneline#label=test-only&
# A `let` read from a body outside the unit was resolved where the body
# is written, but runs on the unit's examples: a nested group's
# `let(:size)` replaces the outer one the outer `let(:sized)` reads. It
# resolves from the unit now.
dead lib Sized#total=test-only&
